# Clean code principles (Unity style guide)

From [Unity's C# style guide](https://unity.com/resources/c-sharp-style-guide-unity-6). Complements `unity-csharp-naming`, `unity-csharp-language`, `unity-csharp-formatting`, `unity-csharp-comments`, `unity-csharp-file-size-limits`, and `unity-common-pitfalls`.

## Single responsibility (SRP)

**Keep classes short.** Each module, class, or method should have **one reason to change** — one clear job.

- Split by concept first; **file length is a hard signal** — see [`unity-csharp-file-size-limits`](unity-csharp-file-size-limits.mdc) (≤300 target, >500 forbidden).
- A Pong-style game gets `Paddle`, `Ball`, `Wall` — not one `GameManager` that moves paddles, bounces the ball, draws UI, and plays audio.
- When a `MonoBehaviour` accumulates unrelated duties (input, scoring, VFX, save/load), **extract types** instead of growing sections or adding `#region` (see `unity-csharp-formatting` class organization).
- Apply SRP to methods too: if a function name needs "And" in the middle, consider a second function or class.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — one type owns unrelated concerns
class GameManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Update() { ReadInput(); MovePaddles(); CheckScore(); }
}

// ✅ GOOD — focused types
class Paddle : MonoBehaviour { … }
class Ball : MonoBehaviour { … }
class ScoreKeeper : MonoBehaviour { … }
```

## DRY — don't repeat yourself

Every piece of knowledge should have **one authoritative place**. Copy-pasted logic diverges; fix bugs once in a shared path.

- Extract **repeated C#** into methods, shared static helpers, base classes, or `ScriptableObject` data — not a third nearly identical `MonoBehaviour`.
- Reuse **Unity assets** (prefabs, materials, animation clips) instead of duplicating hierarchy setup in multiple scenes.
- **Rule of three**: tolerate a single duplication; refactor when the same pattern appears again and the abstraction is obvious.
- **Balance with YAGNI/KISS**: do not build a generic framework for one call site. DRY applies to *stable* duplication, not speculative reuse.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — same damage math in two types
void PlayerHit() { health -= amount * multiplier; }
void EnemyHit()  { health -= amount * multiplier; }

// ✅ GOOD — one implementation
int ApplyDamage(int health, int amount, float multiplier) => …;
```

## KISS — keep it simple

Choose the simplest solution that solves the problem. Avoid unnecessary abstraction, indirection, or cleverness. Computing is hard enough without over-engineering.

## YAGNI — you aren't gonna need it

Implement features when you need them, not when you might need them later. Build the smallest thing that works now.

Reuse Unity and .NET built-ins when they fit (Tilemaps, `UnityEngine.Pool`, etc.) instead of rewriting. The best code is code you do not write.

## Fix causes, not symptoms

Understand the problem before coding. Do not patch around bugs (e.g. a null-guard at the top of a method) without finding why the bad state exists. Ask *why* it happens; trace callers and data flow to the root cause.

## Improve incrementally

Clean code is ongoing maintenance, not a one-time pass. Budget small, regular cleanup. Expect the codebase to drift; prune and refactor as part of normal work.

## Good enough, not perfect

Ship when code meets production standards. Balance new features with cleanup. Refactor when it helps you or the team—not for its own sake.

## Plan, but adapt

Treat the project like a garden, not a fixed blueprint. Designs change as the game grows. Expect to revise, replace, and reorganize code as requirements become clear.

## Be consistent

Once you pick an approach, use it the same way everywhere: naming, casing, folder layout, patterns. Agree as a team, document it (see naming/formatting rules), and follow it in new and touched code.

When editing legacy code, match surrounding style unless you are already refactoring that area.
